<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#vilayet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vilayet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A vilayet was a first-level province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under the administrative system introduced by the Provincial Law of 1864, which replaced the older eyalets. Each was headed by a governor (vali) and divided into sanjaks and smaller districts. The system was applied across the empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and lasted until the end of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vilayet.jpg" alt="Vilayet" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grégoire Aristarchi / Gregory Aristarchis (Bey) / Γρηγόριος Αριστάρχης · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9gislation_ottomane_ou_Recueil_des_Aristarchi-Bey_Gr%C3%A9goire_Tome2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word vilayet is Ottoman Turkish, a loanword from the Arabic wilāya, an abstract noun formed from the verb waliya, to administer. In Arabic it meant province, region or administration in a general sense, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms the Ottoman term was fixed on specific areas in a defined hierarchy. The reform movement was initiated by the Ottoman Reform Edict of 1856.</p><p>The Danube Vilayet was formed in 1864 as an experiment under the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a>, and a vilayet law expanded the system, though it was not until 1884 that it was applied to all of the empire's provinces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> had already begun to modernise its administration and regularise its eyalets in the 1840s. Writing in the Encyclopaedia Britannica in 1911, Vincent Henry Penalver Caillard claimed that the reform was intended to give the provinces more local self-government but in fact centralised more power with the sultan and local Muslims at the expense of other communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Each vilayet was governed by a vali appointed by the sultan, who acted as his representative and was notionally the supreme head of administration in the province. Military administration was entirely separate, though the vali controlled local police. His council included a secretary, a comptroller, a chief justice and directors of foreign affairs, public works, and agriculture and commerce, each nominated by the respective ministers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>; the comptroller answered directly to the finance minister.</p><p>A separate vilayet council had four elected members, two Muslims and two non-Muslims. The hierarchy ran from vilayet to sanjak, kaza and nahiye, under a mutasarrif, a kaymakam and a mudür, down to the muhtar of the village. Similar councils at lower levels included the heads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, the local religious communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the mid-1870s there were vilayets such as Adrianople, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a>, Janina, Aidin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and Tripolitania, together with special cases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>. By 1905 the empire had lost administrative control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Eastern Rumelia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, though all remained under nominal Ottoman sovereignty, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Jerusalem, because of its importance to three monotheistic religions, was an independent sanjak linked directly to the Minister of the Interior.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a> coexisted with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> vilayet without being subordinate to it. In 1917 there were twenty vilayets, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, alongside independent sanjaks. The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> continued to use the term, and its 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 63 vilayets, until it renamed them il in the late 1920s. The term also passed into Albanian, Bulgarian, French and Judaeo-Spanish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vilayet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Land Code and a new penal code followed in 1858, the <b>Vilayet</b> Law and the secular Nizamiye Courts in 1864, and constitutions for the Armenian millet (1863) and the Jewish millet (1865).</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After the opening of the Suez Canal increased the Red Sea's value, Ahmed Muhtar Pasha led an expedition that took Sanaa in 1872, ending the Qasimid State's last remnant and creating the Yemen <b>Vilayet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a></li></ul></section>
